  "Name": "Hass1959-Fig18-MgF2-on-Al-1300A",
  "Source": "G. Hass and R. Tousey, \"Reflecting Coatings for the Extreme Ultraviolet,\" J. Opt. Soc. Am. 49, 593-602 (1959). doi:10.1364/JOSA.49.000593 - Sec. VI p. 601 text and Fig. 18 caption/right panel (Al: n = 0.3, k = 1.0; MgF2: n = 1.6, k = 0; 1300 A); 250 A from Sec. VI p. 600 and Fig. 17",
  "Notes": "Reflectance-enhancing nonabsorbing overcoat (MgF2, n = 1.6) on opaque aluminium at 1300 A, the calculated case of Fig. 18 (right); thickness set to the 250 A MgF2 used in practice\nSpecification: Maximise normal-incidence reflectance of Al at 1300 A with a dielectric overcoat.\nReported: Fig. 18 right (calculated, normal incidence, 1300 A), R vs optical thickness n*d in waves: uncoated Al R = 55 % (dashed line; exact value from n = 0.3, k = 1.0 is 55.4 %); shallow minimum ~42 % near n*d ~ 0.1 lambda; broad maximum ~71-72 % near n*d ~ 0.33 lambda; back to the uncoated value at n*d = 0.5 lambda. This design (25 nm, n*d = 40 nm = 0.308 lambda at 130 nm) sits near the maximum (~71 %). Independent check by me with the same constants: R = 70.9 % at 25 nm, maximum 71.5 % at n*d = 0.34 lambda (27.6 nm), minimum 41.9 % at n*d = 0.09 lambda.\nNotes: Single film on an opaque substrate (Al treated as semi-infinite, no oxide, as in the paper's calculation). Paper plots the curve against film optical thickness; to reproduce Fig. 18 sweep the MgF2 thickness 0-40.6 nm (0-0.5 lambda at 130 nm). The 25 nm thickness is my choice (the practical 250 A MgF2 coating of Sec. VI) - the figure itself has no single design thickness. Paper notes the measured maximum is higher than calculated because n, k of Al are only approximate.\nLayers from Sec. VI p. 601 text and Fig. 18 caption/right panel (Al: n = 0.3, k = 1.0; MgF2: n = 1.6, k = 0; 1300 A); 250 A from Sec. VI p. 600 and Fig. 17; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours R 70.9 % at 130 nm; paper R ~71 % (calculated, read from Fig. 18).",
